# Simple Battery Charging Current Indicator (with single BJT)

This simple circuit shows the charging current level using just one bipolar transistor.

![image alt](https://github.com/VJ-EVJ/Simple-battery-charging-current-indicator-with-single-BJ-Transistor/blob/1753a3f76ce8c06144e071b4e7fd4d00e90a1305/simple%20charging%20indicator.jpg)


A simple way to detect battery charge with reasonable accuracy. 
Here the end of charging is detected when the battery voltage 
reaches 13.6V (-200mV from the supply voltage) and the current 
at that time is ~133mA, then the red LED goes off. The uniqueness 
of me idea is the reduction of the base opening voltage threshold 
by adding a dropout voltage on R2 resistor from voltage divider 
of R2 + R3. The voltage supposedly reduces the base opening 
voltage of the transistor from ~600mV to ~200mV. This is achieved 
by adding a 400mV difference to the transistor base. A 0.4V voltage 
is created with a divider from R2+R3 resistors.


